type Invoice = { id: string; total: number; currency: string; note?: string };
type ApiResponse = { status: string; data?: Invoice[]; error?: { code: string; message: string } };

function pick(row: any, key: any): any { return row[key]; }

function badge(res: ApiResponse): string {
  switch (res.status) {
    case 'loading': return 'Loading...';
    case 'success': return 'Ready';
    case 'error': return 'Failed';
    default: return res.status;
  }
}

function panel(res: ApiResponse) {
  const banner = res.error ? `${res.error.code}: ${res.error.message}` : null;
  const body = res.data
    ? res.data.map(i => `${pick(i, 'id')} ${pick(i, 'totl')} ${pick(i, 'currency')}`).join(', ')
    : null;
  return { badge: badge(res), banner, body };
}

panel({ status: 'error', data: [{ id: 'INV-1', total: 90, currency: 'EUR' }],
  error: { code: 'card_declined', message: 'Card declined' } });
// { badge: 'Failed', banner: 'card_declined: Card declined', body: 'INV-1 undefined EUR' }
panel({ status: 'partial' }); // { badge: 'partial', banner: null, body: null }

scenarios.modelAnswer:
**Diagnosis: three symptoms, three separate type-level causes, no shared one.**

1. *Banner over a live row.* `status: string` is not a discriminant, so `ApiResponse` is one shape whose `data` and `error` are independent optional fields. `{ status: 'error', data: [...], error: {...} }` is a valid value, and `panel` reads the two fields independently — `banner` from `error`, `body` from `data` — so one payload yields both. Nothing is red because the object matches the declaration exactly: the infeasible combination was never excluded.
2. *Raw `partial`.* The `switch` ends in `default: return res.status`, and a `default` that returns a value is exactly what suppresses exhaustiveness. There is no `never` check, and `status: string` gives the compiler no finite case list to compare against, so a new server status flows through untouched.
3. *`undefined` in the row.* `pick(row: any, key: any): any` accepts every key and returns `any`, so `'totl'` is invisible and the template literal stringifies the missing value. The column name stopped being part of the type system.

**The model.** The discriminant has to be a literal union, and each variant owns only its own fields:

```ts
type InvoiceState =
  | { status: 'loading' }
  | { status: 'success'; data: Invoice[] }
  | { status: 'error'; error: { code: string; message: string } };

type InvoiceStatus = InvoiceState['status'];            // 'loading' | 'success' | 'error'
type InvoiceSummary = Pick<Invoice, 'id' | 'total' | 'currency'>;
type InvoicePatch = Partial<Pick<Invoice, 'note'>>;      // PATCH body: no id, no total
type BadgeCopy = Record<InvoiceStatus, string>;          // a map the compiler keeps complete
```

`InvoiceState` is a `type` alias because a union cannot be an `interface`; the leaf shapes (`Invoice`, the error payload) can stay `interface` if you want declaration merging or `extends`, and there is no runtime difference either way. The utility types are not decoration: `InvoicePatch` cannot carry `id` or `total`, so a PATCH handler that forwards the whole entity stops compiling, and `Pick` keeps the contract small instead of leaking every future field the way `Omit<Invoice, 'note'>` would.

**The generic.** Replace `pick` with the key-constrained form:

```ts
function field<T, K extends keyof T>(row: T, key: K): T[K] { return row[key]; }

const total = field(invoice, 'total');  // number, not any
field(invoice, 'totl');
// Argument of type '"totl"' is not assignable to parameter of type 'keyof Invoice'.
```

`K extends keyof T` is what makes `row[key]` legal inside the body and what rejects the typo at the call site; `T[K]` carries the property type back to the caller, which `any` threw away. `K` is inferred from the literal argument, so callers never write type arguments. One limit worth stating: if `T` has an index signature (`Record<string, unknown>`), `keyof T` widens to `string` and the check disappears — keep the helper on concrete shapes.

**The consumer.** Narrow first, use only the fields that exist in that branch, and close the switch with `never`:

```ts
function assertNever(value: never): never {
  throw new Error(`Unhandled status: ${JSON.stringify(value)}`);
}

function badge(res: InvoiceState): string {
  switch (res.status) {
    case 'loading': return 'Loading...';
    case 'success': return 'Ready';
    case 'error':   return 'Failed';
    default:        return assertNever(res);
  }
}

function panel(res: InvoiceState) {
  if (res.status === 'success') {
    return { badge: badge(res), banner: null, body: res.data.map(formatRow).join(', ') };
  }
  if (res.status === 'error') {
    return { badge: badge(res), banner: `${res.error.code}: ${res.error.message}`, body: null };
  }
  return { badge: badge(res), banner: null, body: null };
}
```

`panel` can no longer produce a banner and a body from the same payload: the branches are the only places those fields exist, and `banner` is a hard `null` in the success return.

**Why the compiler now catches what it used to allow.** Both rejected literals produce a named diagnostic (verified on TypeScript 5.9.3):

- `{ status: 'success' }` → `Property 'data' is missing in type '{ status: "success"; }' but required in type '{ status: "success"; data: Invoice[]; }'`.
- `{ status: 'error', data: [...] }` → `Object literal may only specify known properties, and 'data' does not exist in type '{ status: "error"; error: { code: string; message: string; }; }'` — the excess-property check runs against the variant the discriminant selected.

The impossible state stopped being a value the type permits; it became a value the type cannot express. That is the asymmetry with the old model: optional fields describe *absence*, unions describe *alternatives*.

**Why a fourth status cannot ship quietly.** Three independent tripwires: the union needs a new member, `assertNever` rejects it (`Argument of type '{ status: "partial"; }' is not assignable to parameter of type 'never'`), and a `Record<InvoiceStatus, string>` map reports `Property 'partial' is missing` — the same check applies to an object checked with `satisfies Record<InvoiceStatus, string>`. Any one is usually enough; keeping the copy map keyed by the union is what makes the translation reviewable at build time.

**What is still runtime.** Every mechanism above is erased. `JSON.parse` still returns `any`, so a `{ status: 'partial' }` from the network does not become an `InvoiceState` by annotation: one function has to validate the payload (a zod schema or a hand-written guard) and return the narrowed union, so every consumer receives a value whose fields match its discriminant. Types prove the *handling* is complete; they never prove the *incoming data* is. That division is the answer — `assertNever` and `Record` guard the code you control, the parse guards the wire.
